## Key Design Points

| Feature | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Glob matching** | Each task has a pattern matched against both the filename and relative path |
| **Debouncing** | 300 ms cooldown per task prevents duplicate runs from rapid FS events |
| **Any command** | Tasks run arbitrary shell commands with args |
| **Recursive** | Subdirectories are watched automatically |
| **Zero async** | Uses `std::sync::mpsc` — simple blocking channel, no runtime needed |

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <functional>
#include <string>
#include <atomic>
#include <chrono>

// Represents a unit of work
struct Task {
    int id;
    std::string name;
    std::function<void()> work;
};

// Thread-safe task queue with producer-consumer pattern
class TaskQueue {
public:
    void push(Task task) {
        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
            queue_.push(std::move(task));
        }
        cv_.notify_one();
    }

    // Blocks until a task is available or queue is stopped
    bool pop(Task& task) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        cv_.wait(lock, [this] {
            return !queue_.empty() || stopped_;
        });

        if (stopped_ && queue_.empty())
            return false;

        task = std::move(queue_.front());
        queue_.pop();
        return true;
    }

    void stop() {
        {
            std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
            stopped_ = true;
        }
        cv_.notify_all();
    }

    size_t size() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        return queue_.size();
    }

private:
    std::queue<Task> queue_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable cv_;
    bool stopped_ = false;
};

// Pulls tasks from the queue and executes them
class Consumer {
public:
    Consumer(TaskQueue& queue, int id)
        : queue_(queue), id_(id), tasks_processed_(0) {}

    void run() {
        std::cout << "[Consumer " << id_ << "] Started\n";

        Task task;
        while (queue_.pop(task)) {
            std::cout << "[Consumer " << id_ << "] Executing task "
                      << task.id << ": " << task.name << "\n";
            task.work();
            tasks_processed_++;
        }

        std::cout << "[Consumer " << id_ << "] Stopped. Processed "
                  << tasks_processed_ << " tasks.\n";
    }

private:
    TaskQueue& queue_;
    int id_;
    int tasks_processed_;
};

// Manages consumer threads
class Scheduler {
public:
    Scheduler(int num_consumers) {
        for (int i = 0; i < num_consumers; i++) {
            consumers_.emplace_back(queue_, i);
        }
    }

    void start() {
        for (auto& consumer : consumers_) {
            threads_.emplace_back(&Consumer::run, &consumer);
        }
    }

    void submit(Task task) {
        queue_.push(std::move(task));
    }

    void shutdown() {
        std::cout << "[Scheduler] Shutting down. Remaining tasks: "
                  << queue_.size() << "\n";
        queue_.stop();

        for (auto& t : threads_) {
            if (t.joinable())
                t.join();
        }
        std::cout << "[Scheduler] All consumers stopped.\n";
    }

private:
    TaskQueue queue_;
    std::vector<Consumer> consumers_;
    std::vector<std::thread> threads_;
};

// --- Demo ---
int main() {
    Scheduler scheduler(3);  // 3 consumer threads
    scheduler.start();

    // Submit some tasks
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        scheduler.submit({
            i,
            "Task_" + std::to_string(i),
            [i] {
                // Simulate variable work
                std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(100 + (i % 3) * 50));
                std::cout << "  -> Task " << i << " completed\n";
            }
        });
    }

    // Let tasks process, then shut down
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(2));
    scheduler.shutdown();

    return 0;
}
```

## How it works

**Three layers:**

1. **`TaskQueue`** — Thread-safe wrapper around `std::queue` using a mutex + condition variable. `pop()` blocks until work arrives or `stop()` is called.

2. **`Consumer`** — Loops pulling tasks from the queue and executing them. Exits cleanly when the queue is stopped and drained.

3. **`Scheduler`** — Owns the queue and spawns N consumer threads. Provides `submit()` to enqueue work and `shutdown()` for graceful teardown.

**Key design points:**

- **Blocking pop** — consumers sleep instead of busy-waiting (condition variable)
- **Graceful shutdown** — `stop()` signals all consumers; they finish remaining tasks then exit
- **Thread safety** — all queue access is mutex-protected
- **Tasks are generic** — any `std::function<void()>` works as a payload

