## How It Works

```
parseDocument("file.csv", { maxRetries: 3 })
│
├─ Attempt 1
│   ├─ fs.readFile()  ──▶  EBUSY error (file locked)
│   └─ FileAccessError thrown → retryable ✓
│       └─ Wait 500ms + jitter
│
├─ Attempt 2
│   ├─ fs.readFile()  ──▶  EBUSY error again
│   └─ FileAccessError thrown → retryable ✓
│       └─ Wait 1000ms + jitter
│
├─ Attempt 3
│   ├─ fs.readFile()  ──▶  success ✓
│   └─ parseCSV()     ──▶  parsed result
│       └─ Return { filePath, fileType, data }
│
└─ Non-retryable errors (ParseError, ENOENT) fail immediately
```

## Key Design Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **Separate retry utility** | Reusable across any async operation, not coupled to parsing |
| **Retryable vs non-retryable errors** | File-not-found and parse errors won't self-fix — skip them. I/O contention errors can resolve |
| **Exponential backoff + jitter** | Prevents thundering herd when batch-parsing many files |
| **Concurrent batch parsing** | Worker pool pattern limits open file handles while maximizing throughput |
| **Parser registry by extension** | Easy to extend — just add a new entry to `PARSERS` |

