## Usage

```bash
# Add a one-time task
node scheduler.js add "Say hello" "2025-07-15 14:30" "echo Hello World"

# Add a recurring task
node scheduler.js add "Disk check" "2025-07-15 08:00" "df -h" daily

# List all tasks
node scheduler.js list

#   ID   STATUS    NAME                SCHEDULED             REPEAT    COMMAND
#   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   1    pending   Say hello           7/15/2025, 2:30 PM   none      echo Hello World
#   2    pending   Disk check          7/15/2025, 8:00 AM   daily     df -h

# Remove a task
node scheduler.js remove 1

# Clear finished one-time tasks
node scheduler.js clear

# Start the scheduler (foreground process)
node scheduler.js start
# ⏰ Task scheduler started
#    Checking every 30 seconds. Press Ctrl+C to stop.
#
# [2:30:00 PM] Running #1: "Say hello"
#   → echo Hello World
#   Hello World
#   ✓ Done
```

## Key Design Decisions

| Concern | Approach |
|---|---|
| **Storage** | Flat JSON file (`tasks.json`) — no database needed |
| **Dependencies** | Zero — only Node.js built-in modules |
| **Scheduling** | `setInterval` polling every 30s — simple and predictable |
| **Recurring tasks** | Auto-advances `scheduledAt` after each run |
| **Task execution** | `child_process.exec` — runs any shell command |
| **Failure handling** | Marks one-time tasks as `failed`; recurring tasks keep running |

The scheduler runs as a **foreground process** (`start` command). For production use you'd wrap it with `pm2`, `systemd`, or `nohup`.

