## How the Cancellation Flow Works

```
Main Thread                         Worker Thread
───────────                         ─────────────
  │                                      │
  ├── postMessage({ type:'run', id:1 })──►│  starts processing
  │                                      │  checks isCancelled()
  │                                      │  checks isCancelled()
  ├── controller.abort()                 │
  │   ├── postMessage({ type:'cancel' })─►│
  │   └── reject(AbortError)             │  isCancelled() → true
  │                                      │  stops work, cleans up
```

## Key Design Points

| Concern | Solution |
|---|---|
| **Cooperative cancellation** | Worker checks `ctx.isCancelled()` at safe points inside loops |
| **Immediate cancellation** | `AbortController.abort()` instantly rejects the Promise on the main thread |
| **Multiple concurrent tasks** | Each task gets a unique `id`; cancellation targets one task |
| **Cleanup** | Abort listeners are removed on normal completion to prevent leaks |
| **Hard kill (last resort)** | `worker.terminate()` kills the thread and rejects all pending tasks |

> **Note:** Web Workers run on a separate OS thread, so truly long-running synchronous code can only be stopped *cooperatively* (checking `isCancelled()`) or by calling `worker.terminate()`. The `cancel` message approach is preferred because it lets the worker clean up gracefully.

