## Architecture Overview

```
 enqueue()
    │
    ▼
┌─────────────────────────────┐
│  Priority Queues            │
│  ┌──────┐ ┌──────┐ ┌─────┐ │
│  │ HIGH │ │ NORM │ │ LOW │ │
│  └──┬───┘ └──┬───┘ └──┬──┘ │
└─────┼────────┼────────┼────┘
      └────────┼────────┘
               ▼
        ┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐
        │  _poll()    │────▶│  _execute()  │ × concurrency
        │  (loop)     │     │  (worker)    │
        └─────────────┘     └──────┬───────┘
                                   │
                          ┌────────┼────────┐
                          ▼        ▼        ▼
                      success    fail    max retries
                          │        │        │
                       emit()   re-queue   DLQ
```

## Key Design Decisions

| Concern | Approach |
|---|---|
| **Priority** | 3-tier array (high/normal/low), drained in order |
| **Concurrency** | `activeTasks` Map caps parallel execution |
| **Retries** | Exponential backoff + jitter, re-enqueued with `scheduledFor` |
| **Cancellation** | `AbortController` per task, passed into handler via `ctx.signal` |
| **Dead letters** | Tasks exceeding max retries land in DLQ with retry support |
| **Graceful shutdown** | `stop(true)` waits for active tasks; `stop(false)` aborts them |
| **Delayed tasks** | `scheduledFor` timestamp — `_dequeue` skips until ready |

This covers the 90% case for in-process job scheduling. For distributed systems, swap the in-memory arrays for Redis (e.g., BullMQ) or a database-backed store using the same handler/retry patterns.

