## How It Works

```
Main Thread                    Worker Thread
───────────                    ─────────────
 start() ──────────────────▶  execute(token)
    │                              │
    │                         token.reportProgress(25)──▶ onProgressChanged
    │                              │
 cancel()                     token.throwIfCancelled()
    │                              │
    ├─ cancelRequested = true      ├─ CancellationException thrown
    ├─ future.cancel(true)         │
    │                         onCancelled callback fires
    ▼                              ▼
```

## Key Design Decisions

| Concept | Mechanism | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Cooperative cancellation** | `AtomicBoolean` flag | Thread-safe; task checks it at safe points |
| **Interrupt support** | `future.cancel(true)` | Wakes threads blocked on `sleep`/`wait`/I/O |
| **Progress** | `AtomicInteger` + callback | Lock-free, readable from any thread |
| **Daemon thread** | `setDaemon(true)` | Prevents hanging JVM on shutdown |
| **Two cancellation styles** | `throwIfCancelled()` vs `isCancellationRequested()` | Throw = abort immediately; poll = return partial results |

The critical principle is **cooperative cancellation** — Java can't safely force-kill a thread, so the task must periodically check the cancellation token at logical checkpoints in its work.

