## Bug Summary Table

| # | Bug | Fix Applied |
|---|-----|-------------|
| 1 | No `IDisposable` — resource leak | Implemented `IDisposable` with `Dispose()` |
| 2 | `List<T>` not thread-safe | Switched to `ConcurrentDictionary` |
| 3 | No cancellation support | Added `CancellationTokenSource` |
| 4 | Timer fires immediately (0ms delay) | 1-second initial delay |
| 5 | 10ms tick wastes CPU | 1-second tick interval |
| 6 | No input validation | `ArgumentNullException` / range checks |
| 7 | Unsafe collection add | `TryAdd` on concurrent dictionary |
| 8 | `TimerCallback` signature | Kept `object state` — added proper guard |
| 9 | No re-entrancy guard | `Monitor.TryEnter` / `lock` pattern |
| 10 | Iterating while modifying | `.ToList()` snapshot before iteration |
| 11 | `DateTime.Now` (local time) | Changed to `DateTime.UtcNow` |
| 12 | Exact `==` time comparison | Changed to `<=` (never miss a task) |
| 13 | No exception handling | `try/catch` per task + failure counter |
| 14 | `Remove` during `foreach` | Safe removal via `TryRemove` after snapshot |
| 15 | Timer not disposed | `timer.Change(Infinite)` + `Dispose()` |
| 16 | No visibility into state | Added `GetPendingTasks()` |
| 17 | No unique task ID | `Guid` identifier for cancel/track |
| 18 | No recurring support | `RecurrenceInterval` with auto-reschedule |

> **Key Takeaways**: Most scheduler bugs stem from **thread safety** (race conditions on shared collections), **time handling** (`Local` vs `UTC`, exact vs range comparison), and **missing error boundaries** (one failing task shouldn't crash the loop).

