echo "foo baz quick brown fox"      > /tmp/test_watch/b.txt
echo "updated content world"        > /tmp/test_watch/a.txt   # modify
rm /tmp/test_watch/b.txt                                       # delete
mkdir -p /tmp/test_watch/sub && echo "nested file" > /tmp/test_watch/sub/c.txt
```

## How It Works

```
┌──────────────┐       events        ┌─────────────┐
│  inotify fd  │ ──────────────────► │ FileWatcher  │
│  (kernel)    │  CREATE/MODIFY/DEL  │  poll loop   │
└──────────────┘                     └──────┬───────┘
                                            │ callback
                                            ▼
                                     ┌─────────────┐
                                     │ SearchIndex  │
                                     │              │
                                     │ token→{paths}│  inverted index
                                     │ path→{tokens}│  reverse map (for fast delete)
                                     └─────────────┘
```

## Key design points

| Concern | Approach |
|---|---|
| **Watching** | Linux `inotify` — zero polling, kernel-pushed events |
| **Recursive dirs** | Watches added on startup + on `IN_CREATE` of new dirs |
| **Index structure** | Inverted index (`token → set<path>`) for O(1) lookups |
| **Fast removal** | Reverse map (`path → set<token>`) avoids full index scan |
| **Tokenization** | Whitespace split + lowercased + strip punctuation edges |
| **Re-index on modify** | `removeFile` then `addFile` — always consistent |

To add a query interface you'd just call `index.search("foo")` — it returns matching file paths. You could add a stdin read loop or a socket listener alongside the watcher on a second thread.

